The audience is first-time, never-licensed applicants for a Michigan operator's license, reading at a plain-English level, plus newcomers and parents of teen drivers who use the same handbook. This section covers stopping and following distances, the look-ahead rule, headlight and high-beam laws, roadside emergencies, crash duties and reporting, vehicle-deer crashes, trip preparation and mirror adjustment, personal safety and carjacking, disabled vehicles, and conduct during a traffic stop. It teaches how much following distance to keep, when headlights and high beams are required or banned, what to do after a crash or a deer strike, and how to behave when disabled or pulled over. It assumes no prior driving knowledge and no prerequisites beyond literacy, so wording stays concrete, procedural and free of legal jargon throughout.
